# 55. Post-Core Agent Completion and Settings / AI Provider Infrastructure

After Artemis Core reaches its approved completion gate, TitanGold must finish
or re-validate remaining specialized Agent products one at a time before treating
the ecosystem as mature.

Each remaining Agent must still follow Sections 24, 38 and 45–53.

A complete Agent is not merely a backend function. It must be:

- A truthful specialized product for the user.
- A truthful machine-consumable participant in the Artemis architecture.
- Explicit about unavailable data and limitations.
- Explicit about authority.
- Evaluated under its own success metrics.

Once the required Agent products are complete or their deferred state is
explicit, TitanGold must perform a dedicated Settings and AI Provider
Infrastructure Program Slice.

## 55.1 Canonical Settings Ownership

Global AI provider configuration must have one canonical owner.

The preferred product location is the canonical Settings area, including the
Configuration / API Integrations surface or its verified successor.

Before implementation, inspect the real current Settings architecture and
identify the canonical owner. Do not create a second provider-configuration
system merely because Artemis, Agents or another screen needs provider access.

Artemis and Agents should consume read-only status and links to the canonical
owner rather than duplicate provider Secrets or configuration forms.

## 55.2 API Integration Capability Inventory

For every external AI provider or model platform, maintain a capability inventory
that may include:

- providerId
- provider family
- modelId
- model/version identifier
- supported modalities
- reasoning capability class
- tool/function-calling support
- structured-output support
- streaming support
- context constraints
- rate limits
- quota
- cost metadata when available
- latency observations
- region or residency constraints where relevant
- provider availability
- credential status
- health/readiness
- data-retention/privacy classification
- allowed TitanGold use cases
- prohibited use cases
- fallback eligibility
- deprecation state

Provider and model names in documentation are examples, not permanent
architecture.

The canonical registry and current verified provider documentation are
authoritative.

Potential provider families may include, when officially supported and approved:

- OpenAI / ChatGPT-class models
- Google Gemini-class models
- DeepSeek-class models
- Anthropic Claude-class models
- xAI Grok-class models
- Mistral-class models
- Approved local or self-hosted models
- Future providers added through the same canonical contract

Do not hardcode the architecture around one vendor.

## 55.3 Credential and Secret Ownership

All external-AI credentials must follow Section 17.

In addition:

- Secrets have one encrypted server-side owner.
- The frontend never receives reusable Secrets after submission.
- Stored Secrets are never prefilled.
- Provider responses and logs must not echo Secrets.
- Prompt traces must not include Secrets.
- Provider health must not require exposing credentials.
- Credential rotation must be auditable.
- Disabling one provider must not corrupt unrelated providers.

A provider may be configured but not authorized for Artemis use.

Do not conflate:

- provider configured
- credentials valid
- provider healthy
- model available
- task-compatible
- privacy-compatible
- Artemis-approved
- execution-relevant

## 55.4 Model Catalog and Provider Health

TitanGold should maintain one canonical model catalog rather than scattering
model names in frontend components or prompts.

Health/readiness should distinguish:

- configured
- authenticated where required
- healthy
- degraded
- rate-limited
- quota exhausted
- unavailable
- unsupported
- disabled

A single healthy provider or model must not imply that the entire advisory
system is healthy.

Readiness must reflect the requirements of the actual requested workflow.

## 55.5 Routing, Fallback and Cost Governance

External model selection must be task-aware.

Routing may consider:

- task type
- domain
- required modality
- required structured output
- historical quality for that task
- calibration
- current provider health
- latency
- cost
- quota
- privacy classification
- context size
- model/version stability
- diversity from already selected advisors

Do not always call every model.

Do not always call the most expensive model.

Do not choose a cheaper or faster model when it cannot satisfy the required
quality, privacy or safety boundary.

Fallback must be explicit and auditable.

A fallback response must not silently masquerade as the originally selected
model.

Every external-AI Program Slice must define bounded:

- request count
- concurrency
- timeout
- retry
- token/context budget
- cost budget
- cache behavior
- cancellation behavior

Unbounded fan-out to external models is prohibited.

